From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Ask for the larger picture rather than the faster one

Both were watched. Twice the rate at half the size was asked for to see
whether motion came out any smoother, and it did not look different to the
person watching it: the material was a recording made at thirty frames a
second, and nothing sent twice as often can put back what was never there.

It is not free, either. The work done on each frame before it reaches the
encoder is done in software, so twice as many frames is twice as much of it,
and the processor beside the encoder was found two degrees under the point
where it stops itself.

So the larger picture is asked for, at the rate the receiver offers it. The
preference is written out rather than left to the weighing that would arrive
at the same mode, so that what is wanted is stated where it can be changed
rather than inferred from how a list happens to be sorted.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
@@ -258,7 +258,7 @@
   gint height;
   gint refresh_rate;
 } wfd_preferred_modes[] = {
-  { 1280, 720, 60 },
+  { 1920, 1080, 30 },
 };
 
